// test_typecheck_hof_delayed_fwd.mkt
//
// PURPOSE: Verify that a higher-order function call that transitively reads an
// uninitialized variable through its argument closure is correctly rejected by
// the static dependency analysis pass.
//
// EXPECTED OUTCOME: FAILS static checks (dependency analysis detects an eager
// forward reference to `a` which is not yet initialized when `d` is defined).
//
// RATIONALE: `def d = app(fn x => x + a)(c)` eagerly calls `app(...)` during
// initialization. `app = fn f => fn x => f(x + b)`, so the call chain
// evaluates to `(fn x => x + a)(c + b)`, which calls `x + a` with the
// argument closed over `a`. Since `var a = 0` is declared after `d`, reading
// `a` here is a use-before-initialization bug that must be caught.
//
// This is the complex HOF example from GitHub Issue #173 (PR #120 comment
// by @neurusL). Per the issue discussion: "d has read a already by forcing
// thunks -- this is not intended to pass name resolution."
//
// Makefile target: test-forward-decls (expected: exit non-zero, inverted with !)

service s {
    var b = 0;
    var c = 0;
    def app: (int -> int) -> (int -> int) = fn f => fn x => f(x + b);
    def d = app(fn x => x + a)(c); 
    var a = 0;
}
